WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.009
This paper asks a fundamental question in learning
theory: how well does plain logistic

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:10.185
regression solve the problem of agnostically
learning halfspaces?

00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:12.433
A halfspace is a linear classifier,

00:00:12.513 --> 00:00:17.572
and in the agnostic setting an adversary corrupts
an OPT fraction of the labels,

00:00:17.652 --> 00:00:21.941
so the best any linear classifier can do is a
zero-one error of OPT.

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:27.594
Prior work left a wide gap: a lower bound saying no
convex surrogate can beat order OPT,

00:00:27.674 --> 00:00:33.119
and an upper bound showing logistic regression
achieves only order square-root of OPT.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.853
The authors close this gap.

00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:40.506
They construct a well-behaved distribution on which
the global minimizer of the logistic

00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:43.333
risk provably suffers square-root OPT error,

00:00:43.413 --> 00:00:45.838
matching the known upper bound exactly.

00:00:45.918 --> 00:00:51.813
They then show that adding a natural
radial-Lipschitzness assumption lets logistic regression

00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:54.061
reach near-optimal order-OPT error.

00:00:54.141 --> 00:00:57.851
Finally, they give a strikingly simple two-phase
algorithm,

00:00:57.931 --> 00:01:01.063
logistic regression followed by a perceptron step,

00:01:01.143 --> 00:01:05.303
that attains near-optimal error for any
well-behaved distribution,

00:01:05.383 --> 00:01:09.800
replacing the many rounds of optimization that
prior methods required.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.790
The paper studies the agnostic learning of
homogeneous halfspaces,

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:19.480
one of the most fundamental problems in machine learning.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.170
We are given samples from an unknown distribution
over feature-label pairs,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.860
and we want a linear classifier whose zero-one
error is close to OPT,

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.550
the best error achievable by any homogeneous halfspace.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.240
Equivalently,

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.930
an adversary is allowed to flip an OPT fraction of
the labels of an otherwise perfectly linearly separable dataset.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:47.620
Logistic regression is the natural, ubiquitous
heuristic here,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.310
yet its theoretical guarantees for this problem
were poorly understood.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.000
Prior results left a frustrating gap: a lower bound
saying no convex surrogate loss can beat order OPT error,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.690
against an upper bound showing logistic regression
reaches only order square-root of OPT.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:06.380
The central question is which of these bounds
reflects the truth for logistic regression.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:11.870
Logistic regression is arguably the most widely
deployed classification algorithm,

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:16.110
so understanding its statistical guarantees is not
merely academic.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.920
The worry is that logistic regression can behave
terribly in the worst case;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.362
earlier work showed its risk minimizer can be wrong
on nearly a one-minus-OPT fraction

00:02:26.442 --> 00:02:29.590
of examples on an adversarially built distribution.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:34.780
To rule out such pathologies, the community focuses
on well-behaved distributions,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.843
such as isotropic log-concave ones, where much
stronger guarantees are possible.

00:02:39.923 --> 00:02:45.602
But even under these assumptions the precise error
rate of logistic regression was unknown,

00:02:45.682 --> 00:02:49.526
sitting somewhere between order OPT and order
square-root OPT.

00:02:49.606 --> 00:02:54.968
Pinning down this rate tells us whether logistic
regression is a near-optimal agnostic

00:02:55.048 --> 00:02:59.082
learner or whether it fundamentally leaves accuracy
on the table,

00:02:59.162 --> 00:03:02.120
and if so, what minimal fix recovers optimality.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.551
The paper makes three contributions that together
resolve the gap.

00:03:06.631 --> 00:03:10.306
First, it constructs an explicit two-dimensional,
isotropic,

00:03:10.386 --> 00:03:15.626
well-behaved distribution on which the global
minimizer of the logistic risk provably

00:03:15.706 --> 00:03:18.067
attains square-root OPT zero-one error,

00:03:18.147 --> 00:03:23.762
matching the known upper bound and proving that
logistic regression alone cannot do better.

00:03:23.842 --> 00:03:26.954
Second, it identifies a clean sufficient condition,

00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:29.207
radial Lipschitzness of the density,

00:03:29.287 --> 00:03:33.901
under which logistic regression does reach the
near-optimal order-OPT rate.

00:03:33.981 --> 00:03:35.465
Third, and most usefully,

00:03:35.545 --> 00:03:39.721
it introduces a simple two-phase algorithm: run
logistic regression,

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:45.229
then run a perceptron-style hinge-loss step from
that warm start on a restricted domain.

00:03:45.309 --> 00:03:51.988
This attains near-optimal error for every
well-behaved distribution without the radial-Lipschitz assumption,

00:03:52.068 --> 00:03:55.430
at the cost of just one additional convex optimization,

00:03:55.510 --> 00:04:02.440
and it is far simpler than prior algorithms that
required solving a logarithmic number of minimization problems.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.277
The technical core rests on two convex surrogate losses.

00:04:06.357 --> 00:04:10.255
The logistic loss is the log of one plus e to the
minus margin,

00:04:10.335 --> 00:04:14.234
and the hinge loss is the positive part of the
negative margin;

00:04:14.314 --> 00:04:17.897
both are minimized as empirical averages over the samples.

00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:19.160
For the lower bound,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:23.581
the authors hand-design a distribution Q on the
plane with four parts,

00:04:23.661 --> 00:04:27.244
a construction valid when OPT is at most one over sixteen,

00:04:27.324 --> 00:04:32.548
engineered so that the true optimal halfspace is
only wrong on a small noisy region,

00:04:32.628 --> 00:04:39.180
yet the logistic risk minimizer is pulled into a
direction that misclassifies a square-root OPT fraction.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.579
For the upper bound and the algorithm,

00:04:41.659 --> 00:04:47.200
the key object is the angle between the learned
direction and the ground-truth direction.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.526
Phase one runs projected gradient descent on the
logistic risk inside a ball of radius one over square-root epsilon,

00:04:54.606 --> 00:05:00.715
which under well-behaved-ness returns a direction
within angle order square-root OPT of the truth.

00:05:00.795 --> 00:05:05.515
Phase two then runs projected stochastic gradient
descent on the hinge loss,

00:05:05.595 --> 00:05:08.609
which is exactly the classical perceptron update,

00:05:08.689 --> 00:05:12.777
starting from that warm start and confined to a
restricted domain.

00:05:12.857 --> 00:05:18.840
This second convex step sharpens the angle and
drives the zero-one error down to near order OPT.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.568
Because this is a learning-theory paper,

00:05:21.648 --> 00:05:24.724
there are no benchmark datasets or empirical tables;

00:05:24.804 --> 00:05:30.126
the "data" are distribution classes and the results
are theorems with explicit constants.

00:05:30.206 --> 00:05:36.135
The positive results hold for the standard family
of well-behaved distributions used in prior work,

00:05:36.215 --> 00:05:43.055
meaning the marginal on features is isotropic and
satisfies soft-margin and sub-exponential regularity conditions.

00:05:43.135 --> 00:05:50.156
The negative result is carried by a concrete
counterexample: an explicit distribution Q on the two-dimensional plane,

00:05:50.236 --> 00:05:54.223
built from four parts and illustrated in the
paper's single figure,

00:05:54.303 --> 00:05:57.804
that is fully well-behaved yet defeats logistic
regression.

00:05:57.884 --> 00:06:00.414
The upper bounds are stated in two regimes,

00:06:00.494 --> 00:06:04.420
one for bounded feature distributions where the
norm is at most B,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.455
and one for sub-exponential feature distributions,

00:06:07.535 --> 00:06:12.250
so the guarantees span the settings that matter for
isotropic log-concave data.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.852
The headline finding is a matching pair of bounds.

00:06:15.932 --> 00:06:18.824
On the constructed well-behaved distribution,

00:06:18.904 --> 00:06:26.552
the global minimizer of the population logistic
risk has zero-one error at least square-root OPT divided by sixty pi,

00:06:26.632 --> 00:06:32.694
which asymptotically matches the best known upper
bound and therefore proves that square-root

00:06:32.774 --> 00:06:36.062
OPT is the true rate for logistic regression alone.

00:06:36.142 --> 00:06:40.422
That is the sense in which the paper closes the
long-standing gap.

00:06:40.502 --> 00:06:43.856
Then, by adding radial Lipschitzness of the density,

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:48.413
the same logistic minimizer jumps to near-optimal
error of order OPT,

00:06:48.493 --> 00:06:53.036
up to a constant Cκ that is bounded whenever the
density is Lipschitz.

00:06:53.116 --> 00:06:55.150
And without any such assumption,

00:06:55.230 --> 00:07:00.896
the two-phase algorithm achieves order OPT plus
epsilon error for bounded distributions

00:07:00.976 --> 00:07:04.991
and order OPT times log one over OPT for
sub-exponential ones,

00:07:05.071 --> 00:07:06.840
in expectation over the run.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:11.025
Although this is a theory paper without
experimental ablations,

00:07:11.105 --> 00:07:15.711
the analysis is structured to expose which
assumptions buy which guarantees,

00:07:15.791 --> 00:07:17.869
playing the role an ablation would.

00:07:17.949 --> 00:07:23.172
The single upper-bound argument is versatile enough
to recover the earlier square-root

00:07:23.252 --> 00:07:27.242
OPT rate for general well-behaved distributions as
a special case,

00:07:27.322 --> 00:07:32.298
and crucially it also goes through for the hinge
loss, not just the logistic loss;

00:07:32.378 --> 00:07:36.492
this dual applicability is what enables the
perceptron second phase.

00:07:36.572 --> 00:07:40.808
The theorem cleanly separates the bounded and
sub-exponential regimes,

00:07:40.888 --> 00:07:46.111
showing that the only price of moving from bounded
to heavier sub-exponential tails is

00:07:46.191 --> 00:07:48.824
an extra logarithmic factor in one over OPT.

00:07:48.904 --> 00:07:53.449
Likewise, the constant Cκ, an integral of the
radial-Lipschitzness modulus,

00:07:53.529 --> 00:07:57.519
isolates exactly how much the density's radial
smoothness matters,

00:07:57.599 --> 00:08:00.540
vanishing when the density is radially symmetric.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.396
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:08:05.476 --> 00:08:09.952
The lower bound gives a zero-one error of at least
square-root OPT over sixty pi for the logistic minimizer,

00:08:10.032 --> 00:08:14.507
valid whenever OPT is at most one one-hundredth.

00:08:14.587 --> 00:08:19.063
Under radial Lipschitzness, logistic regression
reaches near-optimal order-OPT error.

00:08:19.143 --> 00:08:23.619
The two-phase algorithm achieves order OPT plus
epsilon error for bounded distributions

00:08:23.699 --> 00:08:28.175
and order OPT times log one over OPT for
sub-exponential ones.

00:08:28.255 --> 00:08:32.731
It does so with a sample complexity of order d over
epsilon squared,

00:08:32.811 --> 00:08:37.287
a quadratic improvement over the d over epsilon to
the fourth required by prior nonconvex methods.

00:08:37.367 --> 00:08:41.842
And structurally,

00:08:41.923 --> 00:08:46.398
whereas earlier algorithms that hit order OPT error
had to solve a logarithmic number

00:08:46.478 --> 00:08:50.954
of minimization problems while guessing OPT by
binary search,

00:08:51.034 --> 00:08:55.510
this method needs only two convex steps.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:59.962
The lasting message is twofold.

00:09:00.042 --> 00:09:04.115
First,

00:09:04.195 --> 00:09:08.267
logistic regression by itself cannot beat
square-root OPT error for agnostically learning halfspaces,

00:09:08.347 --> 00:09:12.419
and the paper proves this rate is exactly tight by
constructing a distribution that forces it.

00:09:12.499 --> 00:09:16.572
Second,

00:09:16.652 --> 00:09:20.724
the fix is remarkably cheap: appending one
perceptron-style convex minimization,

00:09:20.804 --> 00:09:24.876
warm-started from the logistic solution and
confined to a bounded domain,

00:09:24.956 --> 00:09:29.028
provably boosts the guarantee to near-optimal
order-OPT error for every well-behaved distribution.

00:09:29.108 --> 00:09:33.181
Along the way the paper also identifies radial
Lipschitzness of the feature density as

00:09:33.261 --> 00:09:37.333
the natural condition under which logistic
regression alone already succeeds.

00:09:37.413 --> 00:09:41.485
In short, a passerby can remember that logistic
regression is half the answer,

00:09:41.565 --> 00:09:45.638
and a single extra convex step completes it,

00:09:45.718 --> 00:09:49.790
more simply and with better sample complexity than
any prior method.
